  "transcript": "# The impact of serious and organised crime on local neighbourhoods\n\n## How big is rural crime, and what does it include?\n> Rural crime is broad, often organised, and badly measured – insurance figures are only the visible tip.\n\nChair: We’re focusing on rural crime today—how organised criminality affects farms and villages.\nRobyn Munt: Farmers see theft, waste crime, hare coursing and intimidation; all disrupt farms, homes and food production.\nDr Kate Tudor: We don’t have reliable national statistics. Rural offences aren’t separated out in police data, so debates rely on NFU Mutual claims.\nDr Smith: NFU Mutual figures show one part—insured theft—but rural serious and organised crime also includes modern slavery, food fraud, agrochemical counterfeiting and cybercrime.\nBen Maguire: Constituents say they stop reporting because cases aren’t followed up. That reduces recorded crime and hides the scale of the problem.\nDr Kate Tudor: Many rural offenders meet definitions of organised crime groups, but because they aren’t mapped as OCGs, the national picture misses them.\nRobyn Munt: Reporting depends on trust with local police; where that relationship is poor, crimes go unreported and impacts—like childcare or crop timing—are greater.\nDr Smith: My rough estimates put potential insurance-relevant losses far higher than single-company figures; we’re looking at much bigger hidden costs.\n\n\n## Policing on the ground: neighbourhood teams, tech and training\n> Good local policing and technology help, but capability varies wildly between forces and needs stable funding and training.\n\nDr Prinsley: Are named neighbourhood officers and local stations useful in rural areas, like fire stations?\nDr Kate Tudor: Named officers help if they’re competent in rural crime. Symbolic stations matter less than investigative capacity and specialist skills.\nDonna Jones: In Hampshire I reintroduced named local bobbies and reopened front counters; visible presence and local bobbies reduced crime in some towns by about 19%.\nDavid Allen: We invested 44% more in neighbourhood policing staff in Cumbria; working with local groups and WhatsApp alerts reduced quad-bike thefts by nearly half.\nRobyn Munt: Technology—drones, deployable CCTV and ANPR—works well when funded and maintained, but many forces rely on short-lived grants.\nDr Smith: Neighbourhood policing builds trust and intelligence. In my surveys, rural confidence in police can be dramatically lower than urban figures.\nAndrew Huddleston: Training is key: not every officer must be an expert, but they must know where to go for specialist rural knowledge. Our National Rural Crime Unit helps train officers nationally.\nBen Maguire: Is there best practice to spread nationally?\nDr Kate Tudor: Yes: dedicated rural coordinators in each force, better intelligence collation and institutionalised rural training would help—but they need funding.\nRobbie Moore: How well do ROCUs support rural cases?\nDr Kate Tudor: ROCU support happens, but ROCUs focus on highest threat/harm. Many rural organised groups fall between local capacity and ROCU thresholds, so they slip through.\nDr Smith: That gap means regional work only reaches the most extreme rural cases, leaving persistent organised groups unaddressed.\n\n\n## County lines, wildlife crime and industry partnerships\n> County lines and organised groups now use rural spaces for a wide range of crimes; industry partnerships are essential to respond.\n\nBell Ribeiro-Addy: How do county lines operate in rural areas now?\nDr Kate Tudor: County lines have shortened their routes and adapted: nominals from those networks commit wildlife crimes—poaching, hare coursing—that bring violence and intimidation to rural communities.\nRobyn Munt: Once groups find a soft spot they return. Members report repeated assaults and day-time trespass affecting crops, livestock and access.\nDr Smith: Rural areas can also host drug production—abandoned barns become low-visibility sites for labs—and reports and evaluations have under-recognised rural roles.\nAndrew Huddleston: Organised crime groups target rural areas because they can move high-value kit and hide it quickly. We saw national GPS thefts and plant thefts run across many forces; rural teams and industry funding were key to catching offenders.\nBen Maguire: Partnerships—like Operation Scorpion—help cross borders. How important are industry partnerships?\nAndrew Huddleston: Crucial. The NFU, Construction Equipment Association and insurers fund training, intelligence sharing and the National Rural Crime Unit’s co-ordination role.\nRobbie Moore: What about the Equipment Theft (Prevention) Act—why hasn’t it reduced thefts yet?\nAndrew Huddleston: Implementation and manufacturer adoption take time. The Act’s measures—like better marking and immobilisation—need rollout and enforcement to deter organised thefts of tractors and quad bikes.\nDr Smith: We must also be careful around licensed cannabis sites; if not secured they can be infiltrated, so licensing must include strong protections.\n\n\n## Policy, funding and police reform: practical asks\n> Panellists want stable funding, better intelligence sharing, targeted legislation and careful design of police reform so rural needs aren’t neglected.\n\nChair: How do you prioritise rural crime among competing urban threats?\nDonna Jones: PCCs set local priorities. I funded a rural crime taskforce, mobile CCTV, drones and named local officers. That needed local investment to change focus.\nDavid Allen: PCCs can convene partners. In Cumbria we use the ARIS fund—seized assets—to buy kit and support community prevention; prevention reduces later demand on policing.\nAndrew Huddleston: Ask: strengthen the Equipment Theft Act roll-out, push for search powers and support national co-ordination so ROCUs, NCA and rural teams can act together.\nDr Kate Tudor: Police reform could help if it builds rural needs into the national operating model, but large mergers risk drawing resources to high-demand urban areas if not carefully designed.\nDonna Jones: I’m not against reform, but very concerned by proposals to cut forces to a handful. Bigger isn’t automatically better for rural coverage or local accountability.\nDavid Allen: Do the middle bit well: centralise IT and specialist functions, keep local accountability, and evolve rather than revolutionise boundaries.\nRobyn Munt: Farmers need consistent responses: better recording of rural crime, trained staff in control rooms and visible outcomes to rebuild reporting and trust.\nDr Smith: Government should rural-proof reform and policies; currently rural crime is mentioned little in national planning documents despite clear harms.\nChair: Final point: what one practical change would you suggest now?\nAndrew Huddleston: Mandate rural crime coordinators in every force, backed by funded regional intelligence channels, to stop criminals exploiting information gaps.\nDonna Jones: Invest in deployable tech and local named officers so communities see prevention and feel safe.\nDavid Allen: Use seized funds and local convening power to boost prevention programmes and restore neighbourhood policing where it works.\nDr Kate Tudor: Improve how rural crime is recorded and shared nationally so policy and ROCU decisions match reality.\nRobyn Munt: Make sure industry partnerships continue and government funds the core policing roles, not just short-term grants.\nChair: Thank you all. We’ll take these recommendations forward in the inquiry.\n",
  "inquiry": "The impact of serious and organised crime on local neighbourhoods",
